## CAVEAT FOR ALL FUTURE BASELINING (important)

B019 makes fixture output depend on WHICH PLUGINS ARE INSTALLED LOCALLY,
because the game's define set does too. A dependency that resolves on this
machine yields a live `#if DEPENDENCY_X` branch; on a machine without it the
branch is masked and its consumers become unused.

Demonstrated: isolating the plugin install so MLHook stops resolving moves
tm-dips-plus-plus 283 -> 290 diagnostics, the 7 added all being 'never used'
warnings for declarations whose only consumers live in the now-masked block.
Correct behaviour — but it means a fixture baseline is no longer a property of
the tree alone.

So: B019-adjacent baselining must PIN THE PLUGIN INSTALL, not just the tree.
Two binaries compared on the same machine at the same time is still a valid
A/B (that is what the merge gate used); comparing against a number recorded
elsewhere or earlier is not.

Related hazard found by the Codex review on this branch:
`check_command_applies_manifest_defines_and_dependency_defines` implicitly
relied on this machine's installed `Editor` plugin — it asserted a
DEPENDENCY_EDITOR branch was live, which after B019 only holds if Editor
resolves. It passed here and would have failed on a clean machine or in CI.
Fixed on the branch by provisioning the dependency in the fixture.

## Test-suite hermeticity verified (lane, 2026-08-30)

Question asked: does any OTHER test share the machine-dependence Codex found
in check_command_applies_manifest_defines_and_dependency_defines?

Method (an experiment, not a grep): cargo test cannot run under a fake HOME
(rustup needs it to locate a toolchain), so the lane built normally, collected
all 16 test binaries via 'cargo test --no-run', and ran each directly with
HOME and USERPROFILE pointed at an empty directory. That simulates a machine
with no Openplanet install AND no user config, since both
~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins (the auto-detected plugins dir) and
~/.config/openplanet-lsp/config.toml are HOME-derived.

Result: all 16 binaries, 1426 tests, 0 failures.

CRITICALLY, the detector was validated before the negative result was
trusted. The pre-fix tree (4ce1e93) was archived to a scratch dir, built in
isolation, and the offending test run both ways:
  real HOME (Editor installed) -> passes
  clean HOME                   -> FAILS
So the method reproduces the known bug, and the clean sweep is meaningful
evidence rather than a vacuous pass.

Answer: no other test in the suite has this dependence.

Stated limits of the claim (both honest and worth keeping):
- It would not catch a test depending on an absolute path OUTSIDE HOME. The
  lane confirmed by reading call sites that every --plugins-dir in the suite
  points inside a temp fixture, so there are none today.
- It cannot catch a test depending on the ABSENCE of a locally installed
  plugin (would fail here, pass elsewhere) — but the suite is green under
  both homes, so no such test exists either.

The Codex fix itself: provisions Editor inside the test's own fixture dir,
points the run at it with an explicit --plugins-dir, and isolates HOME. 15
added lines, test-only, no production code. Landed as dba3178 on top of
4ce1e93; no history rewritten.